INT. UNDERSEA CORAL CATHEDRAL - THRONE HALL - DUSK

Arches of living coral rise into green gloom. Shafts of light pass through a ceiling of slow, shimmering water. Schools of silver fish loop through pillars like banners in a wind. The air, impossibly, is breathable, but everything moves as if underwater: hair, robes, flame.

EMPRESS THALASSA-VEY, 80s, ancient and translucent as a jellyfish, sits on a throne grown from pale branching coral. Her robes drift. At her right stands ADMIRAL CORVIN HASK, 50s, scarred, armored in abalone plates. At her left, the young priestess SELENE OKONKWO-REYES, 20s, clutches a conch shell. At the foot of the dais, a boy, TIDE, 10, sits cross-legged with a dented tin whistle.

EMPRESS THALASSA-VEY
The tide has turned. I feel it in my bones.

ADMIRAL HASK
The fleet awaits your word.

SELENE
There is no fleet, Admiral. There are seven ships and four are leaking.

ADMIRAL HASK
Then three.

The conch glows in Selene's hands. Tide blows a soft note on the whistle. A crab scuttles across the floor.

EMPRESS THALASSA-VEY
Selene, what does the shell say?

SELENE
(listening)
It says the surface is coming down.

ADMIRAL HASK
The surface has always been coming down.

SELENE
Not like this.

A VOICE from deep within the walls, vast and slow, rolls through the hall.

THE DEEP (V.O.)
(echoing)
Empress. The debt is due.

The fish scatter. The light dims. Hask draws a trident. Thalassa-Vey lifts one hand, and the water stills. Tide stops playing.

EMPRESS THALASSA-VEY
I know.

She rises, slowly, and her robes billow out like a sail. She steps down from the throne, past Hask, past Selene, and kneels in front of the boy. Takes the whistle gently from his hands.

EMPRESS THALASSA-VEY (CONT'D)
Child, you will play for me.

TIDE
What song?

EMPRESS THALASSA-VEY
The last one. The one your mother taught you.

Tide takes a breath and begins, small and thin. Hask lowers the trident. Selene lowers the shell. Above them, the sunlight goes out, one ray at a time.
